  >
    Spotlight
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    Berlin backs Sudan
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> Germany hosted an international conference in Berlin to raise funds for <a href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-sudan-aid-conference-in-berlin-raises-13b/a-76786563" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">people affected by Sudan’s civil war</a>. German Foreign Minister Johann Wadephul said donors pledged €1.3B, surpassing last year’s total. Germany announced €212M in additional humanitarian aid, while the UK and Norway also made newer commitments.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Continuous fighting:</strong> Sudan’s war has entered its fourth year with no sign of peace, despite mounting deaths, hunger, and displacement. Fighting between the Sudanese army and the Rapid Support Forces has shattered hopes of a democratic transition after Omar al-Bashir’s fall. The country is divided, with the army holding key northern and eastern areas while the RSF controls much of Darfur and parts of the south and west.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Big picture: </strong>Sudan is no longer just a domestic civil war but a regional and global failure with widening consequences. Its spillover is destabilizing neighboring states through refugee flows, hunger, and insecurity. At the same time, weak diplomacy and shrinking aid budgets show the limits of international crisis response.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
