<mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 0.5px 1px 1px" border-radius="5px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="5px" padding="0px">
    
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    Erasmus is back
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> Britain and the EU have finalized an agreement for the UK to <a href="https://uk.news.yahoo.com/uk-signs-deal-rejoin-eus-015600461.html" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">rejoin Erasmus+ from January 1, 2027</a>, marking a symbolic post-Brexit rapprochement. The initial agreement will run for one year, with Britain contributing £570M toward the scheme in 2027. The UK government says more than 100,000 people could benefit through study, training, apprenticeships, volunteering, youth exchanges, and sport.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Why did they exit?</strong> After formally leaving the EU, the UK had the option to remain in the program by contributing to it, but Boris Johnson’s government decided the expense was too high. Ministers argued the UK was not getting good value because far more EU students came to Britain through Erasmus than British students went abroad. The government instead chose to launch its own replacement, the Turing Scheme, which it said would provide more global opportunities beyond Europe.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What is Erasmus+? </strong>Erasmus+ is the European Union’s flagship education and mobility program, designed to let people study, train, work, volunteer, and take part in exchanges across Europe and partner countries. It began in 1987 as a university exchange scheme but has since expanded to include school pupils, teachers, apprentices, youth groups, sports participants, and adult learners. Participants can spend up to a year abroad while remaining enrolled at their home institution.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <!-- </mj-section> -->
    <!-- <mj-spacer height="10px" /> -->
    <mj-section
