          <h2
            style="
              padding-bottom: 8px;
              color: #06266d;
              text-align: left;
              border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d;
              font-size: 26px;
              line-height: 1.2;
              font-weight: 300;
            "
          >
            Analysis
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <!-- <mj-text
          padding="10px 12px"
          font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
          color="#000000"
        > -->
        <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;" color="#000000">
<h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
  Turkey digs deep
</h2></mj-text><mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>
<mj-text padding="0px 12px 20px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000"><p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> Turkey has begun a major <a href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-is-somalia-allowing-turkey-to-tap-its-oil-reserves/a-76790840" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">oil exploration push in Somalia</a>, sending its deep-sea vessel Cagri Bey to waters off Mogadishu to drill to depths of 7,500 meters in three large abroad blocks. The move follows a wider Turkey-Somalia defense and economic partnership and is being framed by Somali leaders as a potential turning point for a country long held back by conflict and underused natural resources.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Building trust: </strong>Turkey first built trust in 2011 by delivering large-scale humanitarian aid during a devastating drought. From there, it expanded into hospitals, infrastructure, education, and business, gradually becoming one of Somalia’s most visible foreign partners.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;">Security cooperation deepened the relationship further, with Turkey training Somali forces, opening its largest overseas military base in Mogadishu, and later signing a 10-year defense and economic pact. By the time oil entered the picture, Turkey had already positioned itself as a reliable ally, investor, and protector.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Concerns:</strong> Critics worry Somalia may become too dependent on Turkey. Some analysts argue that Turkey sits too close to Somalia’s strategic assets and revenue flows, making the partnership unequal. There are also fears about how future oil wealth will be managed in a fragile state with a history of conflict, weak institutions, and political divisions. If revenues are mismanaged, the project could fuel corruption or instability instead of development.</p></mj-text>
        <!-- </mj-text> -->
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
